WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.010
- Hi everyone, welcome
to our daily homeroom.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:32.590
I'm very excited about
the guests we have today

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.080
and before we we jump into
that conversation I will give

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.950
my standard announcement.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:38.910
Remind everyone that Khan Academy is

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.730
a not-for-profit organization.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:41.730
That we could only exist

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.620
with philanthropic donations
from folks like yourself.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.620
So if you're in a position
to do so please think

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.900
about donating to Khan Academy.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.640
I wanna give a special shout
out to several corporations

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.000
who've helped support us
especially when they found out

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.190
that we're running at a
deficit pre-COVID and then

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.130
because of COVID our
server costs have gone up,

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:59.590
we've been trying to accelerate
a whole series of programs.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.940
So special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:05.100
AT&amp;T, Fastly and Novartis.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.690
So with that I'm really
excited to introduce

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.210
our next guest, James Manyika.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:12.300
I have many connections
and there's many reasons

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.730
why James is interesting.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:17.840
I consider him a friend, a
mentor, he is also a board member

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.800
of Khan Academy and his day
job is he is the Head of

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.113
the McKinsey Global Institute.

00:01:24.113 --> 00:01:26.930
So James thanks for joining us.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:30.330
May be a good place to start
is for those who don't know,

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.673
McKinsey or the McKinsey
Global Institute, what is that?

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.400
- Well thank you Sal I'm
so delighted to be here.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.520
It's one of those situations
where I feel like you're

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:41.950
one of my teachers.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.260
It's kind of like when you
teacher invite you to class

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.290
and you're kind of terrified

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:46.570
because they've been your teacher,

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.710
so I'm kind of a little
terrified right now

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.180
but I'm delighted to be here.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.730
- James is being so humble.

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.730
He knows which way the mentorship
goes in this relationship.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.630
- No but, so thank you for having me.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.100
So the McKinsey Global Institute

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.010
is kind of McKenzie's economics
and research, think-tank

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:04.843
if you like.

00:02:04.843 --> 00:02:08.850
So we research trends in
the economy, the impact

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.180
that technology is having on society.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:15.040
We look at issues around
economic growth, inequality,

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.630
globalization, so these
are trends we spend

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:21.860
a lot of time thinking about
and we try and make our views

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.820
kind of benefit leaders
where there's leaders

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.330
in the private sector, in
business, in non-profits

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:30.980
in government, many of those folks

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.930
around the world take advantage
of the research that we do.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.290
- So what I'm hoping to
talk to James about I think

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:39.750
there's many things we
could talk to him about

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.160
but directly related to his
work and he obviously sits on

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.180
the board of Khan Academy
and several other foundations

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.130
I'd love to dive into, even
pre-COVID James has done

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:52.270
a lot of interesting work around
the future of work and what

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.420
will technology and automation do to it.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.930
There's then a layer that
COVID has added to it,

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.760
then there's a layer of the
environment we're in around

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:05.060
the anti-racism movement,
how do we increase diversity,

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:08.860
equity and inclusion in
corporations and then what things

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:12.300
might transcend beyond
the COVID crisis or beyond

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.690
the new cycle that we happen to be in.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.520
So if anyone has any questions
on YouTube or Facebook

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.250
please put your questions
in the message boards.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:20.840
We have team members
who are looking at them

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:24.100
and they'll surface those
questions to myself and James.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.660
But James just to get everyone
grounded, given the work

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:27.570
that you just described that

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.789
the McKinsey Global Institute does

00:03:29.789 --> 00:03:33.380
what, even pre-COVID, what
trends have you seen or

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.110
are you all prognosticating
about the future of work?

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:37.420
What is automation going to do?

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:40.120
What is globalization or
de-globalization going to do?

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:45.560
- Well I think work has been
changing and will continue

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.940
to change in some profound
ways and I think there are

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.650
a few different things that
are driving those changes.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:54.760
The first one is the role of
technology and automation.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.710
We know that this moment this is a trend

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:02.120
that's been going on
for centuries actually.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:05.030
Every time we've invented new
ways or technological ways

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:09.100
to complement what people
do, the work that people do

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.340
there's always been this
ongoing kind of tension

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:15.100
and coevolution between what
people do and what the machines

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:17.270
around them to do, so
the role of technology

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:21.350
and automation is one of those
trends that affects work.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:24.950
The other trend that's been
affecting work is the fact that

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:28.130
there are now many more varieties of work.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:30.350
So we know that there are
people who do full-time jobs,

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:32.960
there are people who
now do independent work,

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:34.260
the gig economy.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:37.980
So the set of arrangements
that we put around work

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.900
have kind of expanded.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.690
Some of that has been
driven by the fact that

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.790
some of these alternative
models are flexible for people,

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.410
useful for people, allow people
to complement their incomes

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.780
when they're not earning enough
from their full-time jobs.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:56.500
So this variety of work
phenomena has also been changing

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:57.950
our work quite a bit.

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:02.640
So these were trends Sal
that were already underway

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:04.707
even before COVID.

00:05:04.707 --> 00:05:06.630
They had already impacted sectors

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:11.070
like manufacturing, so if
you had gone to a factory

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:16.070
in 1980 versus a factory in
2018 it looked very different.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:20.978
You probably wouldn't
see as many people there.

00:05:20.978 --> 00:05:23.030
You'd probably seen a lot of robots

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:25.720
and machines assembling
things on the assembly line.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.770
So this was already changing.

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:31.030
But I think one of the things
that as we look forward

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:34.160
that's become important
is that with the emergence

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.350
and progress in artificial
intelligence for example,

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.430
we're starting to see machines
that are capable of doing

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:42.810
some of those things
are we often associated

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.200
with human intelligence.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:46.800
The ability to understand patterns,

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.320
the ability to think through some

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.350
kinds of classes of problems and so that,

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:56.640
the expansion and emergence
artificial intelligence has made

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:00.110
many people start to
worry about, so therefore

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.120
what will people do in the future.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.440
So that's been one of the
trends we've been looking at

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.420
along with the varieties of work.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.770
I should point out
though, sorry you're about

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:09.653
to ask something.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:11.453
- No no no keep going.

00:06:11.453 --> 00:06:14.160
I was gonna ask, all the
things you alluded to,

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.710
automation, artificial
intelligence, there's people

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:20.800
who can paint a very dystopian
future that most jobs

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:21.633
could get taken away.

00:06:21.633 --> 00:06:24.520
I mean people talk about
self-driving cars and trucks

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:27.764
and even a lot of office work or even

00:06:27.764 --> 00:06:30.930
AIs that can do deep learning
algorithms that are better

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:34.223
at diagnosing radiological
images better than radiologists.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.880
Do you all see a future where
we have this mass displacement

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:43.390
of tens of millions of
people even in just the US

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:45.030
or do you think it's more gradual

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:47.300
and we shouldn't be so scared?

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:50.610
- Well we shouldn't be so
scared because one of the things

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:54.873
that actually happens is
that and I'll illustrate this

00:06:54.873 --> 00:06:55.790
with a story.

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:59.420
So, you're a student of history Sal

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.890
and, so, many people may not know this but

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:06.480
in the 60s President
Lyndon Johnson put together

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.470
a commission which is a Blue
Ribbon Commission to look at

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:12.290
the future of work and
the role of automation.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:16.340
One of the conclusions
in their report in 1967

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:20.310
where some of that's
always stuck with me and

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:25.310
what that conclusion was
that technology destroys jobs

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.560
but not work.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:29.910
That is actually quite
important 'cause what happens

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.930
as we've seen both historically
but also looking forward

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:35.180
is that, yes there will
be some occupations

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:36.203
that will decline.

00:07:37.403 --> 00:07:40.320
We don't have people driving
horse and buggies anymore.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.220
We don't know if people
operate elevators anymore

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:45.460
except maybe in a few remaining places.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:49.890
So some jobs will decline
but many more actually grow

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.900
and emerge because there's still
work that needs to be done.

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:56.810
So when you look at the
puts and takes if you like

00:07:56.810 --> 00:07:59.120
much of the research
we've done and in fact

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:02.260
many other economists have done
and technologists have done

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:04.900
and he's talking of looking
at the next few decades

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:09.370
and these suggest that yes
while some jobs will be lost

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:14.000
there will also be jobs gained
meaning jobs that will grow

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.940
and emerge as a result of
new things that we'll do

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:18.010
in the economy.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:21.060
So I like to think of this as
what we see with technology

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:24.760
and automation is a phenomena
of three kinds actually.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:29.340
Yes some jobs lost, some jobs gained

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:32.260
but also many more jobs changed.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:35.150
And the jobs changed
part is quite important.

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:40.150
Think about the things you
spend your day doing Sal.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.140
Most occupations consist

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:47.140
of 20 30 different constituent activities.

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:49.440
Some of those are being replaced by

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:50.760
or those constituent actives

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:52.970
have been replaced by technology,

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:54.540
your job is still there.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:56.550
You mentioned the radiologists.

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:59.430
If you look at what the
radiologists actually does

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:03.370
the amount of time that
they spent looking at images

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:07.050
is I think the number in
the US is something like 38%

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:09.740
is the time to actually
spend looking at images

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:12.177
there are other things that they do.

00:09:12.177 --> 00:09:15.260
They are coordinating things,
they're doing research,

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:17.030
they're doing a whole
bunch of other things.

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:22.030
So that's an example where a
portion of their work changes.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:25.470
Think about the bank teller now which is

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:27.170
a fascinating example.

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:31.110
If you had looked at a bank
teller in 1970 what did

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:33.470
they spend the majority
of their time doing?

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:36.420
Counting your money,
either to take it from you

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.743
when you give it to them
or to give it back to you

00:09:38.743 --> 00:09:40.490
when you ask for it.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:42.730
That was like 80% of their time.

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:46.380
Then the ATM showed up but you
don't need them to do that.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:47.820
Do we still have bank tellers?

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:48.653
We do.

00:09:48.653 --> 00:09:49.790
What do they do?

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:51.110
Quite different activities.

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:53.060
So if you look at the
amount of time bank tellers

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:55.290
are spending counting money

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:57.270
it's a tiny portion.

00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:59.370
The rest of the time they're
doing customer service,

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:03.170
they're helping you think
about accounts you wanna open,

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:04.530
a whole bunch of things.

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:08.060
That's an example of a job
that's changed because of

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:09.560
the advent of technology.

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:13.270
So we'll see this phenomenon
of jobs lost, jobs gained

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:16.880
and jobs changed as in
important thing to keep in mind

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:20.693
as you think about the impact
of technology on our society.

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:24.020
- And what do you think, well
there's a bunch of questions

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:26.820
and even a few that
I'm seeing from YouTube

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:30.920
of just do a synopsis of some
of these is I think everyone,

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:33.410
I hear you but there's a general feeling

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:36.210
that artificial intelligence
might be different than just

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:38.460
the traditional forms of automation that

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:40.540
have happened before and that even if

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:45.180
it doesn't net kill work
does it change the equality

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:48.070
or inequality and this kind
of starts going into what

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:51.240
we're seeing with COVID, what
we're seeing with a lot of

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:55.150
the issues that COVID
might have only highlighted

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:58.540
around inequality, whether
it's around income around race

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:03.320
should we be afraid and is
kind of a lot of the tension

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.180
we're feeling almost
a byproduct of some of

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.230
these trends starting to really hit home.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:12.420
- I think the question is an
important one to think about

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:17.420
because, well as I said before
I worried less about other,

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:19.320
is there gonna be work in the future?

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:21.560
'Cause I think there
will be net of the things

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:22.870
I've just described.

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:24.840
But there is something
I worry about, these are

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:27.140
a couple things I do worry about.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:31.920
I do worry about impact
on incomes and wages

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:35.200
because, I'll illustrate
this as an example Sal.

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:40.200
Many of the hardest automate
occupations tend to be

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:43.890
so think about care work, for
example, it's very very hard

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:45.443
to fully automate that.

00:11:46.474 --> 00:11:49.690
Think about some of the hardest,
many of those occupations

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:52.670
have tended to be
occupations that the wages

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:55.740
have not been great for actually.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:59.700
So there's a worry that
even if we have enough work

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:03.490
it's possible that many of
the jobs won't pay very well

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:06.260
and that's something I do worry about and

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:09.360
that would actually
exacerbate the challenge that

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:12.470
we already have with inequality.

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:14.203
It's something that people
have worried about for

00:12:14.203 --> 00:12:15.036
a long time.

00:12:15.036 --> 00:12:19.820
You often hear politicians
say, we need good jobs back

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:21.320
Manufacturing jobs are good jobs,

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:25.610
it is true manufacturing
jobs have historically paid

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:27.440
quite well but that's
also where we've seen

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:30.010
a lot of automation,
whereas many of the jobs

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:33.270
that have grown in the service
sector have tended to be

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:35.960
not all of them but many of
them have tended to be jobs

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:37.530
that don't pay as well.

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:40.553
So yes the the income inequality
question is clearly one

00:12:40.553 --> 00:12:42.410
that we should worry about.

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:45.260
The other one we should
worry about are the skills

00:12:45.260 --> 00:12:48.600
and adaptation that people
are gonna have to go through.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:53.550
So even if there is enough
work and people work alongside

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:55.490
the machines, that's gonna require

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:58.160
slightly different skills,
slightly different mix of skills

00:12:58.160 --> 00:12:59.900
so how do we retrain everybody?

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:02.540
How do we keep people skills adaptable

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:05.250
as they end up working
alongside technology?

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:08.420
So these issues of skills and adaptation

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:12.540
and the income questions
are things that do worry me.

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:15.065
I worry less about, will there be jobs?

00:13:15.065 --> 00:13:17.510
I worry more about, will they
be the right kind of jobs

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:19.540
and how do we tackle equality?

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:24.110
Now many of these issues we've
often talked about them Sal

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:26.553
as the future of work.

00:13:26.553 --> 00:13:29.190
Well in some ways COVID
has kind of accelerated

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:34.190
all of that and it's now
kind of here and maybe

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:35.830
that's something we should talk about it.

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:38.923
What are we seeing around
work in this moment.

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:40.693
- Right.

00:13:40.693 --> 00:13:42.280
I mean there's from Facebook
we have this question

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:45.230
from Matthew Kavanagh, is AI
making it impossible to predict

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:47.270
the future of work and work patterns?

00:13:47.270 --> 00:13:49.480
Several people have kind of
alluded to what should they do

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:51.860
with their life I'm assuming
they're their high school

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:54.110
or college students and
you're absolutely right.

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:58.120
COVID has, there's kind of
these two worlds people like me

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:01.160
and you who can for the most
part continue to do our work

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:03.260
from our home offices
and then a lot of people

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:05.970
in service industries and
wherever else where they can't

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:08.920
and it's made inequality maybe worse.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:12.830
So what advice would you give
and what trends do you think

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:15.590
from COVID are real and that we really do

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:17.280
have to struggle with.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:19.650
- Well, I think it's important
before we get to what we do

00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:23.740
about it to actually fully
understand what's been exposed

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:25.640
with regards to work and inequality

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:27.190
by this COVID moment.

00:14:27.190 --> 00:14:32.190
So right now for example if
you look at the US workforce,

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:36.140
something like about 1/3 workers that

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:38.560
I would call vulnerable
and let me describe

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:39.820
what I mean by vulnerability.

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:41.730
These are workers who are vulnerable

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:46.730
to layoffs or to being furloughed

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:51.240
or experiencing reduced hours
and income as a result of both

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:54.790
either public safety concerns
about health or the fact that

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:57.960
their sector that they work
in has essentially shut down.

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:00.680
So for example if you happen
to work in their restaurant

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:04.670
for example or in the travel
industry, where there isn't

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:06.840
a whole lot going on right now.

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:09.810
So a third of the US
workforce that's vulnerable

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:10.643
in that sense.

00:15:10.643 --> 00:15:11.610
That's a very large number.

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:16.480
Now if you look at the profile
of who those workers are

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:21.480
it's something like 80% of them
are low wage workers meaning

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:24.980
that they earn less than $40,000 a year.

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:27.700
So it's disproportionately impacting

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:30.560
low-income workers the most.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:34.410
And a large portion of them
don't have college degrees

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:38.520
or college education, 80% of
them actually and many of them

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:40.530
are disproportionately people of color,

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:41.910
black and brown people.

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:46.910
So, if there was already
inequality in an educational sense

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:51.590
or in an or wage sense or in
a kind of demographic sense

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:53.930
we're seeing it in full-blown force.

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:56.720
And those are people as you said Sal

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:00.500
many of those occupations
you can't do them remotely

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:03.070
in the way you and I can do what we do

00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:07.080
because we know that
something like almost 45% of

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:10.530
those vulnerable workers,
their work has to be done

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:13.580
in some physical place somewhere.

00:16:13.580 --> 00:16:16.310
You can't do it over ZOOM
or whatever technology

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:17.390
you happen to be using.

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:18.890
So that's a real concern.

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:21.720
So the question is what
do we do about that?

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:23.390
I think lots of things.

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:27.960
First was to solve for the
public health concerns because

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:30.550
a lot of the people who are most exposed

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:33.340
in the public health
sense have tended to be

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:35.570
many of those same workers.

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:37.960
We also have to, so we have to solve

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:39.560
for their health safety.

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:41.950
Give them the right
equipment PPE and so forth

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:45.180
and making sure that
they're protected if they

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:47.390
have to go out to do work.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:49.700
But the other thing is to think
about how do we supplement

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:52.130
their incomes and help
them and support them?

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:54.020
So some of the stimulus checks

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:58.460
or the unemployment insurance
checks have helped a lot.

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:00.700
But one of the things
we're realizing is that

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:05.400
because we have a system
where a lot of the access

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:09.170
to health services or
benefits are tied to employers

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:12.570
well if employers shut
things down we've essentially

00:17:12.570 --> 00:17:15.230
potentially cut off people
from some of the health

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:17.300
and other benefits that
they might have got.

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:20.310
So that's exposing the
difficulty when we've got

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:24.360
a benefit system that's
inherently tied to employment.

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:26.830
So these are some of the
things we have to tackle

00:17:26.830 --> 00:17:30.523
if we're gonna support those
exposed and vulnerable workers.

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:33.240
- And do you think there's some limit to

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:34.410
I mean this is goes into more from

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:36.550
the macro economics question
that I've asked other guests

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:39.910
but trillion here trillion
there starts to add up

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:40.743
at some point.

00:17:41.780 --> 00:17:44.050
What do you think is the limit at which

00:17:44.050 --> 00:17:46.690
the government won't
be able to write checks

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:48.290
to hold people over essentially?

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:53.790
- Well this is the classic
difficulty of balancing

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:55.883
the short term and the long term.

00:17:57.140 --> 00:18:00.680
So in the short term sense I
would say let's help people

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:03.550
as much as we can because
that's the challenge

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:05.500
that's here and now.

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:08.090
Those are workers we need to
support an economy, (mumbling)

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:12.230
support who are exposed the
public health sense right now

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:15.210
and also in an economic sense right now.

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:18.130
So we have to protect their
lives and livelihoods.

00:18:18.130 --> 00:18:19.790
This is something we've
been writing a lot about.

00:18:19.790 --> 00:18:23.430
How do we protect lives and
livelihoods in this moment.

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:27.320
But it also then has this
other long-term consequence

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:31.880
which is because the way the
economic system gets to write

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:35.380
those checks or trillion
here trillion there is that

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:38.860
in some ways we're borrowing
from the future if you like

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:39.730
in a way.

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:44.730
So this is the tough combination
but personally I would say

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:46.230
let's tackle the issues that are right

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:48.550
in front of us right now which is people

00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:51.060
who are exposed, whose lives are exposed

00:18:51.060 --> 00:18:53.020
and whose livelihoods are exposed.

00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:55.660
So anything we can do to help them I think

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:57.163
is fundamentally important.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:00.660
- Right, right, and what
I've, the general sense

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:02.870
I've been hearing and even
when I think about myself is

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:05.880
the Fed is aggressive that
helps lower interest rates,

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:08.450
they're essentially printing
money and then the government

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:12.670
can borrow at record low interest
rates and really the risk

00:19:12.670 --> 00:19:16.970
in the short to medium term is
inflation which doesn't seem

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:19.100
if anything deflation because
of all the other trends

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:22.143
you talked about like automation,
it might be a bigger risk.

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:27.430
- Oh it would be so in fact
one of the other challenges

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:31.060
we're facing by the way
as I mentioned there's

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:32.863
a lot of workers were exposed right now,

00:19:32.863 --> 00:19:36.840
they're vulnerable because
they can't work and so forth.

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:40.680
Well we know ready that something like 40%

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:43.700
of those occupations also our occupations

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:47.110
that are at risk of automation.

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:50.810
So the interplay of these dynamics

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:51.970
is really really important.

00:19:51.970 --> 00:19:55.010
But I think one of the things
many of us are concluding

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:57.860
is that we kind of have
two simultaneously solve

00:19:57.860 --> 00:20:00.860
these two things which
is, how do we deal with

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:04.320
the public health crisis that
we've got at the same time

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:07.500
we're finding safe ways
to keep the economy

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:10.250
and reopen the economy
and those two things

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:12.010
kind of go hand to hand.

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:15.720
I think the challenge we've
got practically is that

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:19.370
we're running 50 experiments
if you like in fact more

00:20:19.370 --> 00:20:22.900
than that in the sense that
every state is kind of doing

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:25.050
their own thing in fact
it's more than that

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:27.630
because even within states
you find some cities

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:30.840
some counties are doing things
a little bit differently.

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:34.600
That actually makes it very
complicated and in fact raises

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:39.580
the potential that we're gonna
be kind of in this bumpy road

00:20:39.580 --> 00:20:43.500
for a while and recovery will
probably take even longer

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:45.230
because we're kind of
waiting on the one hand

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:49.300
for the public health crisis
to abate or be under control

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:52.408
and waiting for vaccines and
you had Bill Gates talking

00:20:52.408 --> 00:20:53.400
about that, we're waiting

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:57.160
for therapies but also we
need safe ways to reopen

00:20:57.160 --> 00:21:00.688
and we're trying to reopen and
get the economy, so I worry

00:21:00.688 --> 00:21:05.120
that we're gonna be in this
mode of kind of small steps,

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:09.300
bumpy steps back and forth
and forward hopefully for

00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:11.683
quite a while, for longer
than we would like.

00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:13.743
And that's a worry.

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:16.080
- And then related to this and I do think

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:17.310
it is somewhat related.

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:19.440
I don't think it's a coincidence
that we're seeing the

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:23.020
kind of mass protests
in, when you already had

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:25.980
this COVID happening in the background

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:29.420
but you've had obviously
the murder of George Floyd

00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:31.730
which has kind of reinvigorated

00:21:31.730 --> 00:21:33.420
or really opened up conversations

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:36.700
around systemic racism, police brutality,

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:39.840
I'm curious your point of
view obviously personally,

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:43.340
how it affects you and your
family but also as someone

00:21:43.340 --> 00:21:46.993
who thinks systematically
about business and jobs,

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:51.220
there's a fear that a
lot of companies are,

00:21:51.220 --> 00:21:53.720
they do lip service to
the notions of diversity

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:55.890
and inclusivity but at the end of the day

00:21:55.890 --> 00:21:58.750
behind closed doors will say
well but it's a harder problem,

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:01.050
they'll talk about the
pipeline problem or it starts.

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:03.020
Maybe it's Khan Academy's
problem 'cause we have to start

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:05.560
with students when they're
in pre-k in elementary school

00:22:05.560 --> 00:22:06.950
and all that.

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:07.890
How do you think about it?

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:09.180
I mean how are you think about it?

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:12.810
Personally, what do you think
has to change so that we don't

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:15.720
keep repeating this, and
how do you think the role

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:17.710
of the business community, what
action should they be taking

00:22:17.710 --> 00:22:19.163
to not just do lip service?

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:23.260
- Well these are really big
questions Sal as you know

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:26.480
but you asked about the personal,
my own personal experience

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:28.750
and I've lived in the United
States now for about 20 years

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:33.130
and I can tell you these issues affect me

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:34.860
and affect everyone.

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:38.790
One of the things that's
been surprising to me is the,

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:41.900
even when people understand
things there's the presumption

00:22:41.900 --> 00:22:45.543
that oh it happens to
those people over there,

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:49.170
poor disadvantaged people of color

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:50.790
but I think it happens
to all people of color.

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:53.020
So let me give you some
personal experience.

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:56.480
It doesn't matter, I'm fairly privileged,

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:59.870
I'm lucky to do the things
that I do and so forth but

00:22:59.870 --> 00:23:01.680
that doesn't stop the police stopping me

00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:03.940
occasionally, which happens.

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:08.640
It doesn't stop showing up
at work in Silicon Valley,

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:12.920
showing up at a company to say
I'm here to meet with the CEO

00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:15.370
and I'm told, oh you can
leave it here, right?

00:23:15.370 --> 00:23:17.768
The presumption being there
to deliver something, right?

00:23:17.768 --> 00:23:20.820
So, it happens to all of us.

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:25.820
There isn't a a black male
in this country who apart

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:30.080
from people perhaps who are
recognizably celebrities,

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:33.900
there's the black male I
know, CEO down to the janitor

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:37.980
who hasn't got a story to
tell you about some experience

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:39.320
that they've had in the store somewhere,

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:40.560
so this happens to all of us

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:42.630
and we're constantly thinking about that.

00:23:42.630 --> 00:23:46.860
I worry about I'm the
father of a young black man.

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:48.510
I worry about it when he goes out.

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:50.400
So these are very personal issues.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:54.070
But I think zooming out from
the personal, I think perhaps

00:23:54.070 --> 00:23:58.190
what we're seeing is, these
have been issues that have been

00:23:58.190 --> 00:24:02.920
there for a very long time
and it's a combination I think

00:24:02.920 --> 00:24:05.773
perhaps this moment was a
bit of a boiling over point

00:24:05.773 --> 00:24:08.590
with the death of George Floyd
that kind of brought things

00:24:08.590 --> 00:24:12.410
to a fall, but these issues of inequity,

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:15.900
systemic inequities, both in the, not just

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:17.860
in the policing and
criminal justice system

00:24:17.860 --> 00:24:21.330
but in society,
economically, have persisted

00:24:21.330 --> 00:24:22.830
for a very long time.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:26.930
And I think what I'm hopeful
is that maybe this time things

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:30.200
will be different and people start to take

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:32.020
address them directly.

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:34.880
I've been quite encouraged, we'll see,

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:37.680
but I've been encouraged by
the fact that there's been

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:42.680
a bit of a collective chorus
of recognition of these issues

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:46.580
not universal of course but
at least a larger chorus

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:49.650
than before, and I see it both in the,

00:24:49.650 --> 00:24:53.210
inside large companies
in the private sector,

00:24:53.210 --> 00:24:56.520
in foundations and in the social sector.

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:59.490
So that chorus I think is encouraging.

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:03.630
I'm also seeing it go beyond just a chorus

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:06.200
but people starting to propose actions

00:25:06.200 --> 00:25:07.400
that they're gonna take.

00:25:08.997 --> 00:25:10.550
There's some easy actions.

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:12.040
Important but easy actions.

00:25:12.040 --> 00:25:16.160
The easy actions I think for
companies is to write a check.

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:18.300
So donate to this cause and that cause

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:19.860
and fund this fund that.

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:22.140
That's great, it's happening and I think

00:25:22.140 --> 00:25:23.940
many will benefit that way.

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:28.320
The harder thing is to
actually change organizations.

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:32.950
Change for example leadership,
change attitudes towards

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:37.720
these issues, change hiring
practices, address some of the

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:40.120
biased questions that affect candidates

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:42.620
when they're applying for jobs and work.

00:25:42.620 --> 00:25:45.950
Those are much harder things to do.

00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:48.970
But I think people are
committing to doing those so

00:25:48.970 --> 00:25:51.850
I'm hopeful, I'm optimistic in that sense.

00:25:51.850 --> 00:25:53.800
- And just even from your
vantage point as one of

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:56.790
the senior leaders at
McKinsey which is for those

00:25:56.790 --> 00:26:00.040
who don't know McKinsey it
is kind of considered kind of

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:03.280
the elite of the elite in terms
of strategy consulting firms

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:07.210
and you all are the advisors
to CEOs and governments

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:08.520
around the world.

00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:10.930
So a lot of ways you'll
have influence with a lot of

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:13.560
other entities and then
also what y'all obviously do

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:15.160
with your own organization,

00:26:15.160 --> 00:26:18.280
do you see that there
there will be change?

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:19.640
And what are those levers?

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:21.710
If you have to pick
your top three levers on

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:22.543
any of these issues.

00:26:22.543 --> 00:26:25.030
Obviously there's the
police brutality issue which

00:26:25.030 --> 00:26:27.010
we might have limited control over.

00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:30.610
That's kind of a sheriff
government police union type issue.

00:26:30.610 --> 00:26:33.110
But in the corporate sector
what levers would you?

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:36.790
- Well I think in the corporate sector

00:26:36.790 --> 00:26:39.670
it's committing to the kind of inclusivity

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:40.503
that we're talking about.

00:26:40.503 --> 00:26:43.790
I mean for those who are
interested even we at McKinsey

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:46.950
have actually put out 10
actions that we're gonna take

00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:50.710
and some of the harder
ones in the private sector

00:26:50.710 --> 00:26:53.810
for many of us is to make
sure we recruit differently,

00:26:53.810 --> 00:26:57.470
we hire differently, we
address issues of bias

00:26:57.470 --> 00:26:59.770
and help people progress.

00:26:59.770 --> 00:27:03.710
We make sure people are being
mentored and have allies.

00:27:03.710 --> 00:27:05.973
I mean this is one of those things where,

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:13.210
the people of color can't just
work on this by themselves.

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:16.660
I think there's a sense of a
need for collective ownership

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:19.030
of the fact that we do
want to hire differently.

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:21.680
We do want to look at
candidates across the board.

00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:23.850
We do wanna look at people's skills.

00:27:23.850 --> 00:27:27.400
We do wanna look at how do
we support people progress?

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:30.310
Those are some of the more
kind of cultural things

00:27:30.310 --> 00:27:31.460
to think about.

00:27:31.460 --> 00:27:34.630
I think there's some
wonderful work and research

00:27:34.630 --> 00:27:36.740
that was done by Claude Steele.

00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:40.280
So Claude was the provost
at Berkeley and Colombia.

00:27:40.280 --> 00:27:42.800
Before that he was a
professor at Stanford.

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:47.460
About this issue of stereotyping
and bias in how we coach

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:49.350
each other, support each other.

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:52.130
I mean you're a teacher
and you're educating people

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:57.130
but even the way a learner
and their teacher or if it's

00:27:57.630 --> 00:28:00.860
a worker, a worker and their
supervisor who's coaching

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:03.630
and mentoring them, paying
attention to these things

00:28:03.630 --> 00:28:05.450
makes enormous difference.

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:07.070
It makes enormous difference.

00:28:07.070 --> 00:28:10.010
So many corporations and
we're doing it ourselves

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:12.330
going through training
about systemic bias,

00:28:12.330 --> 00:28:13.480
how do you watch for that?

00:28:13.480 --> 00:28:16.480
How do you make sure in
the coaching and mentoring

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:19.670
you're not inadvertently
kind of biasing the feedback

00:28:19.670 --> 00:28:20.810
and the learning?

00:28:20.810 --> 00:28:23.560
That's the hard work that
we're all gonna have to do

00:28:23.560 --> 00:28:25.850
and we at McKinsey I know have

00:28:25.850 --> 00:28:28.030
as much work to do as anybody.

00:28:28.030 --> 00:28:29.940
We don't have enough
black leaders at McKinsey.

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:33.090
We don't have enough people
of color coming through

00:28:33.090 --> 00:28:33.950
in the organization.

00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:35.500
We're all working hard at it.

00:28:35.500 --> 00:28:40.240
So we've made a commitment to
double for example leadership

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:45.240
in our firm at all levels
in four years or less.

00:28:45.260 --> 00:28:47.650
That's gonna be hard for us.

00:28:47.650 --> 00:28:49.833
But that's a big commitment we're making.

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:52.023
- Yeah.

00:28:52.023 --> 00:28:54.750
And going back to I mean
there's a ton of questions

00:28:54.750 --> 00:28:56.660
that are coming in and these
conversations always go faster

00:28:56.660 --> 00:28:58.750
than I expect 'cause there's
so much to talk about.

00:28:58.750 --> 00:28:59.820
I mean you know there's a couple

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:00.950
of questions people are asking.

00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:02.800
I mean going back to
the work question about

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:03.633
what should people do

00:29:03.633 --> 00:29:05.610
with their lives given the uncertainty?

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:07.000
There's there's questions about,

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:07.870
what do you think are going to be

00:29:07.870 --> 00:29:10.470
the permanent effects of COVID

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:13.370
but I would extend it,
COVID and a lot of what

00:29:13.370 --> 00:29:14.540
we just talked about.

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:17.390
There's a very particular
question from Facebook about

00:29:17.390 --> 00:29:22.029
what do you think about Trump's
H1B policy, the Visa policy.

00:29:22.029 --> 00:29:25.540
I'd like to take it where
you'd like to take it James

00:29:25.540 --> 00:29:27.810
in the few minutes we have left.

00:29:27.810 --> 00:29:30.410
- Yeah, so it's worth
talking perhaps about

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:33.030
what's gonna be permanent post-COVID maybe

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:35.740
as trends that people can
anticipate as people think about

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:38.620
what to do, how we adapt and adjust.

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:41.290
I think we've seen ourselves go through

00:29:41.290 --> 00:29:43.870
some rapid global experiments.

00:29:43.870 --> 00:29:45.610
We've experimented with
the work from home.

00:29:45.610 --> 00:29:48.590
We've experimented from remote learning

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:49.703
and people have, more people

00:29:49.703 --> 00:29:51.480
that are already using Khan Academy

00:29:51.480 --> 00:29:55.350
have discovered Khan Academy,
(laughing) thankfully,

00:29:55.350 --> 00:29:57.120
in this moment and
that's a wonderful thing

00:29:57.120 --> 00:30:00.660
but I think many of those
things aren't gonna change.

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:03.420
I think we've seen the benefits
of some of those things.

00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:06.300
We've seen the the value some people have

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:08.480
from working from home, the flexibility

00:30:08.480 --> 00:30:10.320
but some people wanna go back to work.

00:30:10.320 --> 00:30:12.230
So the workplace itself I think

00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:14.260
is gonna look quite different.

00:30:14.260 --> 00:30:15.370
The learning environment I think

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:17.920
is gonna look quite different.

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:21.850
I think we've learned that
we may be in a no contact

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:24.250
or at least a low contact economy.

00:30:24.250 --> 00:30:27.220
I think some of the experiences
of social distancing

00:30:27.220 --> 00:30:30.210
and avoiding density,
I'm not sure all of those

00:30:30.210 --> 00:30:31.270
will go away.

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:36.270
I think we've seen a growth
in telehealth, for example.

00:30:36.330 --> 00:30:38.880
People being more comfortable doing

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:41.180
health consultations remotely.

00:30:41.180 --> 00:30:43.780
I think much of that is gonna stay.

00:30:43.780 --> 00:30:46.020
So those are things that I
think are gonna be with us

00:30:46.020 --> 00:30:47.330
for a while.

00:30:47.330 --> 00:30:51.260
To the question of how
can one adapt and learn?

00:30:51.260 --> 00:30:54.790
I think, it's one thing I
love about Khan Academy,

00:30:54.790 --> 00:30:56.470
I think that being in a mode where

00:30:56.470 --> 00:31:00.470
we are perpetually learning
and augmenting our skills

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:04.030
and what we know, I think
it's just gonna become

00:31:04.030 --> 00:31:06.430
a lifelong skill.

00:31:06.430 --> 00:31:09.990
Learning to learn and
learning to adapt permanently

00:31:09.990 --> 00:31:12.170
and perpetually is one thing.

00:31:12.170 --> 00:31:15.610
The other thing I would
also add is, we're gonna see

00:31:15.610 --> 00:31:20.610
the importance of both skills,
not just the STEM skills

00:31:20.900 --> 00:31:23.078
but also even some of the artistic skills

00:31:23.078 --> 00:31:24.880
and I think it's gonna be the combination

00:31:24.880 --> 00:31:28.070
of these other social skills
with some what some people

00:31:28.070 --> 00:31:30.943
have called STEAM, right,
by adding A to STEM,

00:31:32.356 --> 00:31:33.330
A for the arts.

00:31:33.330 --> 00:31:36.510
I think that combination
is gonna be quite important

00:31:36.510 --> 00:31:40.620
and those who have both kinds
of combinations of skills

00:31:40.620 --> 00:31:42.610
I think are gonna do well.

00:31:42.610 --> 00:31:45.430
So those would be the
two things I'd emphasize.

00:31:45.430 --> 00:31:49.240
Constantly learning and having
this duality between STEM

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:51.561
and artistic and social skills I think

00:31:51.561 --> 00:31:53.680
it's gonna be quite important.

00:31:53.680 --> 00:31:55.890
- Yeah, and I always find
STEAM entertaining because

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:58.302
it really just means being
a well-rounded human being.

00:31:58.302 --> 00:31:59.150
(both laughing)

00:31:59.150 --> 00:32:00.770
'Cause it's covering everything.

00:32:00.770 --> 00:32:03.760
- Exactly, exactly, exactly.

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:06.270
- Well James, unfortunately
we're all out of time

00:32:06.270 --> 00:32:08.140
considering you're a board
member I consider it your duty

00:32:08.140 --> 00:32:09.153
to do this again.

00:32:10.584 --> 00:32:13.670
(laughing) Hopefully we can
have more of these conversations

00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:15.360
'cause I think we just
started scratching the surface

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:17.210
on a lot of this especially
even the future of work

00:32:17.210 --> 00:32:18.610
et cetera et cetera, so,

00:32:18.610 --> 00:32:20.780
thank you James so much for
joining and I hope we do

00:32:20.780 --> 00:32:23.220
do get to do this in
another couple of weeks.

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:27.280
- Well thank you for having
me and, welcome support

00:32:27.280 --> 00:32:29.060
from those watching Khan Academy.

00:32:29.060 --> 00:32:31.713
It's a wonderful wonderful
resource, so thank you.

00:32:32.860 --> 00:32:33.693
- Thanks James.

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:37.390
So thanks everyone for joining.

00:32:37.390 --> 00:32:40.760
As you can see just another
fascinating conversation.

00:32:40.760 --> 00:32:42.530
James is a big hero of mine and someone

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:44.230
I definitely look up to
and is a great board member

00:32:44.230 --> 00:32:45.660
for Khan Academy.

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:48.440
And I look forward to not only
bringing him on in the future

00:32:48.440 --> 00:32:50.900
but we have many other really
interesting guests coming up

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:54.800
and I will see you at future live streams.

00:32:54.800 --> 00:32:55.700
See you all later.

